## Production Rust Editor Architectures: Helix vs Zed

Based on my analysis of both codebases, here's a detailed comparison of how these production editors structure their Rust architectures at scale:

---

## 1. Document/Buffer vs Editor/View Separation

### **Helix Architecture**

Helix maintains a clear layered separation through multiple crates:

**[helix-core](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-core/src)** - Data layer
- Pure text operations: `Rope`, `Transaction`, `ChangeSet`
- Selection logic: `Selection` with `anchor` and `head` positions (char offsets)
- No editor state, no UI coupling

**[helix-view](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-view/src)** - View/Document layer
- [`Document`](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-view/src/document.rs#L141-L220): Owns text state, per-view selections, language server connections, diagnostics
- [`View`](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-view/src/view.rs#L29-L100): Viewport state, scroll position, jump list per view
- [`Editor`](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-view/src/editor.rs): Global editor registry managing all documents/views

```rust
// helix-view/src/document.rs
pub struct Document {
    pub id: DocumentId,
    text: Rope,
    selections: HashMap<ViewId, Selection>,  // Per-view selections!
    view_data: HashMap<ViewId, ViewData>,
    path: Option<PathBuf>,
    syntax: Option<Syntax>,
    language_servers: HashMap<LanguageServerName, Arc<Client>>,
    // ... diagnostics, history, etc.
}
```

**[helix-term](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-term/src)** - UI/Event loop layer
- [`Application`](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-term/src/application.rs#L70-L82): Top-level compositor, terminal, jobs
- Command handlers, keymaps, UI components

**Key design**: Selections are **stored per (Document, View) tuple**. This naturally supports multi-view editing.

---

### **Zed Architecture**

Zed uses a different layering with tighter coupling but explicit multi-buffer support:

**[text crate](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/text/src)** - Low-level text operations
- [`Buffer`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/text/src/text.rs#L51-L60): Core text with history, operation queue
- [`Anchor`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/text/src/anchor.rs#L9-L50): **Timestamped position** with `Lamport` clock, offset, bias, buffer_id
- [`Selection`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/text/src/selection.rs#L17-L64): Generic `Selection<T>` with id, start, end, reversed, goal

```rust
// crates/text/src/anchor.rs
pub struct Anchor {
    pub timestamp: clock::Lamport,      // CRDT-friendly for collaboration
    pub offset: usize,
    pub bias: Bias,                     // Left or Right bias for insertions
    pub buffer_id: Option<BufferId>,
}

// crates/text/src/selection.rs
pub struct Selection<T> {
    pub id: usize,
    pub start: T,
    pub end: T,
    pub reversed: bool,
    pub goal: SelectionGoal,
}
```

**[language crate](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/main/crates/language/src)** - Language support, diagnostics, syntax

**[multi_buffer crate](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/multi_buffer/src/multi_buffer.rs#L69-L97)** - Multi-buffer abstraction
- Treats multiple `Buffer` instances as "excerpts"
- Manages `ExcerptId`, path mappings, diff states
- Single abstraction for "editing multiple files in one view"

```rust
// crates/multi_buffer/src/multi_buffer.rs
pub struct MultiBuffer {
    snapshot: RefCell<MultiBufferSnapshot>,
    buffers: HashMap<BufferId, BufferState>,
    excerpts_by_path: BTreeMap<PathKey, Vec<ExcerptId>>,
    diffs: HashMap<BufferId, DiffState>,
    singleton: bool,  // Single buffer = single excerpt
    history: History,
}
```

**[editor crate](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/editor/src/editor.rs)** - View/UI layer
- [`Editor` struct](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/editor/src/editor.rs#L1024-L1212): Massive struct (1200+ lines) combining view logic with buffer management
- [`SelectionsCollection`](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/editor/src/selections_collection.rs#L27-L37): Manages disjoint selections + pending selection
- `buffer: Entity<MultiBuffer>`
- `display_map: Entity<DisplayMap>` for rendering logic
- `selections: SelectionsCollection` for multi-cursor state

**Key design**: Selections use **Anchors** (timestamped) to remain stable across edits and collaborate. Editor couples buffer + view logic into single entity.

---

## 2. Message/Event/Command Patterns

### **Helix: Event-Driven Hook System**

Helix uses [helix-event](https://github.com/helix-editor/helix/blob/main/helix-event/src/lib.rs) - a custom event dispatch system:

```rust
// helix-event/src/lib.rs
//! Events are tuples of data that trigger synchronous hooks
//! Async hooks run on channels for background work

// Declarative event syntax
events! {
    DocumentDidOpen<'a> {
        editor: &'a mut Editor,
        doc: DocumentId
    }
    DocumentDidChange<'a> {
        doc: &'a mut Document,
        view: ViewId,
        old_text: &'a Rope,
        changes: &'a ChangeSet,
        ghost_transaction: bool
    }
    SelectionDidChange<'a> { doc: &'a mut Document, view: ViewId }
    DiagnosticsDidChange<'a> { editor: &'a mut Editor, doc: DocumentId }
}

// Synchronous hooks run immediately
register_hook!(move |event: &mut DocumentDidChange<'_>| {
    // Modify editor state directly
    Ok(())
});

// Async hooks for expensive operations
impl helix_event::AsyncHook for SignatureHelpHandler {
    type Event = SignatureHelpEvent;
    fn handle_event(&mut self, event: &SignatureHelpEvent, _: &App) { }
}
```

**Flow:**
1. Synchronous hooks run immediately with mutable editor access
2. Async hooks receive events via channels, spawn tokio tasks
3. Results come back via `dispatch_callback()` to main loop

**Strengths:**
- Clean event terminology (past tense: "DidChange")
- Hooks can't interfere with each other (no reentrancy)
- Type-safe event registration

---

### **Zed: GPUI Action-Based System**

Zed uses its own [GPUI framework](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/tree/main/crates/gpui) for actions:

```rust
// crates/editor/src/editor.rs uses #[derive(Action)]
pub struct SplitSelectionIntoLines {
    pub keep_text_selected: bool,
}

impl Editor {
    pub fn handle_action(&mut self, action: &MyAction, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        // Handles specific action types
        // Can emit Events that other components observe
    }
}

// Entity-based dispatch
workspace.register_action(Editor::new_file);

// Context provides async capabilities
cx.spawn(|cx| async {
    // LSP request
    let result = lsp.definition(position).await;
    editor.update(cx, |editor, cx| {
        // Update editor state with result
    });
})?
```

**Flow:**
1. Actions dispatched to focused entity (Editor)
2. Entity handles action synchronously
3. Entity emits Events that Views observe
4. Async work spawned as tasks with closure access

**Strengths:**
- Integrated with entire UI framework
- Actions are statically typed with `#[derive(Action)]`
- Task-based async with context passing

---

## 3. Async Operations (LSP, File I/O)

### **Helix: Channel-Based + Job Queue**

**LSP Handling:**
```rust
// helix-view/src/editor.rs - Event stream
pub async fn next_event(&mut self) -> Option<EditorEvent> {
    futures_util::select! {
        Some(config_event) = self.config_events.1.recv() => {
            EditorEvent::ConfigEvent(config_event)
        }
        Some(message) = self.language_servers.incoming.next() => {
            EditorEvent::LanguageServerMessage(message)
        }
        // ... more events
    }
}
```

**Job System:**
```rust
// helix-term/src/job.rs
pub type JobFuture = BoxFuture<'static, anyhow::Result<Option<Callback>>>;

pub struct Job {
    pub future: JobFuture,
    pub wait: bool,  // Wait before exiting?
}

pub struct Jobs {
    pub wait_futures: FuturesUnordered<JobFuture>,
    pub callbacks: Receiver<Callback>,
    pub status_messages: Receiver<StatusMessage>,
}

pub enum Callback {
    EditorCompositor(EditorCompositorCallback),
    Editor(EditorCallback),
}

// Async hook spawns job that returns callback
pub async fn dispatch(job: impl FnOnce(&mut Editor, &mut Compositor) + Send + 'static) {
    JOB_QUEUE.wait().send(Callback::EditorCompositor(Box::new(job))).await
}
```

**Pattern:**
- Main loop polls `select!` over multiple event streams
- Long-running tasks return `Callback` closures
- Callbacks executed on main loop with full editor access
- Decouples async work from editor mutation

---

### **Zed: Task-Based with Entity Mutation**

**LSP Integration via Tasks:**
```rust
// crates/editor/src/editor.rs (simplified)
pub struct Editor {
    // ... state
    completion_tasks: Vec<(CompletionId, Task<()>)>,
    code_actions_task: Option<Task<Result<()>>>,
    linked_editing_range_task: Option<Task<Option<()>>>,
}

// In handlers
cx.spawn(|mut cx| async move {
    let response = project.definition(location).await?;
    editor.update(&mut cx, |editor, cx| {
        editor.handle_definition_response(response, cx)
    })?;
    Ok(())
})?;
```

**File I/O:**
```rust
// Built into Buffer/MultiBuffer with subscriptions
buffer.on_transaction(|transaction| {
    // React to changes
});

// Async save operations
buffer.save(path).await?;
```

**Pattern:**
- Spawn tasks with `cx.spawn()` from handlers
- Task captures editor reference, calls `.update()` with closure
- Closure gets `&mut Editor` and can modify state directly
- Entity-based subscription system for observing changes

---

## 4. Multi-Cursor and Selections

### **Helix: Selection per View**

**Storage:**
```rust
// helix-view/src/document.rs
pub struct Document {
    selections: HashMap<ViewId, Selection>,  // Multiple cursors per view
    view_data: HashMap<ViewId, ViewData>,
}

// helix-core/src/selection.rs
pub struct Selection {
    // Multiple ranges can be active
    ranges: SmallVec<[Range; 1]>,
    primary_index: usize,
}

pub struct Range {
    pub anchor: usize,      // Start of selection
    pub head: usize,        // Cursor position
    pub old_visual_position: Option<(u32, u32)>,
}
```

**Operations:**
- Selections are immutable values updated via `Selection::map()`
- Commands take all ranges and return transformed ranges
- Natural for Kakoune-style editing (all selections execute in parallel)

---

### **Zed: Anchor-Based Disjoint Collections**

**Storage:**
```rust
// crates/editor/src/selections_collection.rs
pub struct SelectionsCollection {
    next_selection_id: usize,
    line_mode: bool,
    disjoint: Arc<[Selection<Anchor>]>,      // Non-overlapping selections
    pending: Option<PendingSelection>,         // In-progress drag/selection
    select_mode: SelectMode,
    is_extending: bool,
}

pub struct MutableSelectionsCollection<'snap, 'a> {
    collection: &'a mut SelectionsCollection,
    snapshot: &'snap DisplaySnapshot,
    selections_changed: bool,
}
```

**Key features:**
- **Anchors** (timestamped) persist across edits - critical for collaboration
- **Disjoint** constraint: no overlapping selections
- **Pending** selection for mouse drag operations
- **Bias** in anchors: controls which side of insertion point selection moves

```rust
// Zed's approach to keeping selections stable
pub struct Anchor {
    pub timestamp: clock::Lamport,  // CRDT-style versioning
    pub offset: usize,
    pub bias: Bias,                 // Left = moves left, Right = moves right
    pub buffer_id: Option<BufferId>,
}
```

**Operations:**
- Selections converted to display points only when needed for rendering
- Anchors automatically adjust when text changes
- Used for stable cross-edit references (especially collaboration)

---

## Architecture Comparison Matrix

| Aspect | Helix | Zed |
|--------|-------|-----|
| **Crate Separation** | 6+ crates (clear layers) | 3-4 large crates (tighter coupling) |
| **Selection Storage** | `HashMap<ViewId, Selection>` per Document | `Arc<[Selection<Anchor>]>` in SelectionsCollection |
| **Anchor Type** | Char offset only | Timestamped with Lamport clock + bias |
| **Multi-View** | Native (selections per view) | Via MultiBuffer excerpts |
| **Event System** | Custom hooks + async channels | GPUI actions + entity mutations |
| **Async Pattern** | Job queue returns callbacks | Task-based with entity.update() |
| **Selection Semantics** | Immutable ranges (Kakoune-style) | Mutable anchors (stable across edits) |
| **Collaboration** | Not built-in | Native with timestamped anchors |
| **View/Buffer Coupling** | Loose (separate concerns) | Tight (Editor owns MultiBuffer) |

---

## For Elm-Style Architecture at Scale

**Will it scale?** Analysis suggests:

**Helix's approach is more compatible with pure Elm-like immutability:**
- Clear separation enables easier state machine reasoning
- Event system doesn't require global mutable state
- Selections as immutable values
- Would need explicit command queuing for async

**Zed's approach trades Elm-purity for performance:**
- Entity mutations directly modify state (not functional)
- Timestamps on anchors enable CRDT consistency without full immutability
- Task-based async requires careful closure capture
- Built-in collaboration at architectural level

**Key takeaway**: At production scale (100k+ LOC), both editors chose **targeted mutability** over pure immutability—but Helix maintains better separation of concerns while Zed optimizes for collaboration and rendering performance.
